  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ヘンナ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%98%E3%83%B3%E3%83%8A",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 108609494,
      "text": "ヘンナ（ヘナ）は、ミソハギ科の植物の名。和名は、指甲花（シコウカ）・ツマクレナイノキ・エジプトイボタノキ。学名は、Lawsonia inermis。主に、マニキュアやヘナタトゥーなどの染料として古代から使用されてきたハーブである。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Lawsonia inermis」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawsonia_inermis",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1365740417,
      "text": "Lawsonia inermis, also known as hina, the henna tree, the mignonette tree, and the Egyptian privet, is a flowering plant and one of the only two species of the genus Lawsonia, with the other being Lawsonia odorata. It is used as a traditional medicinal plant. The species is named after the Scottish physician Isaac Lawson, a good friend of Linnaeus."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "ja": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ヘンナ」の節「栽培」",
        "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%98%E3%83%B3%E3%83%8A#%E6%A0%BD%E5%9F%B9",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 108609494,
        "heading": "栽培",
        "text": "ヘナの木は北アフリカ、アジア、オーストラリア北部などの半乾燥帯と熱帯に自生する。35℃から45℃の間で育ったヘナが最も染まりが良いとされる。降雨のある間、ヘナは早く生長し、新枝を出すが、長引く乾燥や涼しい天候においては葉が徐々に黄色になり、落ちてしまう。11℃以下の環境では生長ができず、5℃となると死んでしまう。",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Lawsonia inermis」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawsonia_inermis#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1365740417,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "It produces the most dye when grown in temperatures between 35 and 45 °C (95 and 113 °F). During the onset of precipitation intervals, the plant grows rapidly, putting out new shoots. Growth subsequently slows. The leaves gradually yellow and fall during prolonged dry or cool intervals. It does not thrive where minimum temperatures are below 11 °C (52 °F). Temperatures below 5 °C (41 °F) will kill the henna plant.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
